    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `publish_run` in `app/publish.py` and added a test for it. `pytest -q` passes (2 tests). Nothing was uploaded to the real Hub; the test uses a fake API that only records the calls.\n\n**Which function it uses:** the huggingface_hub version pinned here (2.0.0) no longer has `upload_large_folder`, so the code uses `upload_folder`. According to its docstring, in 2.0.0 `upload_folder` splits large folders into several commits to stay under the Hub's limits. If an upload is interrupted, running the same call again skips files that were already committed. This relies on `hf_xet`, which is installed in `.venv` (1.6.0), so nothing new needs installing. Without `hf_xet`, it would fall back to a single commit that can fail on very large folders. It would also do that if `HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET` is set on the training machines.\n\n**What the function does:**\n- It creates `repo_id` as a private dataset repo, or uploads into it if it already exists.\n- It uploads the whole of `run_dir` to the root of the repo, skipping `*.tmp` files at any depth and everything under the top-level `wandb/` folder.\n- Like `publish_summary`, it takes an optional `api` argument, which the test uses to pass in the fake.\n\nThe new test checks the repo-creation and upload settings. It also runs the skip rules through huggingface_hub's own file matcher to confirm which files get left out. It can't test batching or resuming, because those only happen against the real Hub."
  },
